//? Title: Goldschmidt division: square the gap, inspect the rounding
//? Subtitle: Actual native states, selected operation counts and finite-precision limits
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Division can be approximated by repeatedly multiplying a numerator and
denominator by the same correction, steering the denominator towards one. This
report derives the exact-arithmetic recurrence, then follows the library's
actual double-precision calculation. Native records expose both products and
their shared factor without replay. The original four examples remain, alongside
controls showing denominator stagnation and loss during normalization. A small
denominator gap is not a certificate of a correctly rounded quotient.
)

\toc

> Begin with two exact updates

To divide 3 by 4, normalization gives N₀=3/8 and D₀=1/2. The first factor is
3/2; both products are exact binary fractions. The numerator product can start
as soon as the factor exists, independently of the denominator product. This
data-flow observation does not measure speed or imply that this implementation
launches parallel work.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/goldschmidt_flow.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/goldschmidt_division.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto t = dm::algorithms::goldschmidt_trace(3, 4, 2, 2);
    std::print("| update | N before | D before | factor | N after | D after |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: " "| ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto &q : t.steps)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", q.update,
                   q.before.numerator, q.before.denominator, q.factor,
                   q.after.numerator, q.after.denominator);
    const auto &q = t.steps.front();
    dm::graphics::Diagram d(65, 1100);
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;
    box.width = 4.6;
    box.height = 1.6;
    box.font_size = 22;
    d.node("factor", {0, 0},
           std::format("f = 2 - D\n2 - {} = {}", q.before.denominator, q.factor),
           box).node("numerator", {7, 2},
                     std::format("N × f\n{} × {} = {}", q.before.numerator,
                                 q.factor, q.after.numerator), box).node(
        "denominator", {7, -2},
        std::format("D × f\n{} × {} = {}", q.before.denominator, q.factor,
                    q.after.denominator), box).edge("factor", "numerator").edge(
        "factor", "denominator").text({3.5, 4},
                                      "One shared factor; two independent product dependencies",
                                      23).save_svg("build/goldschmidt_flow.svg");
    if (t.run.quotient != .703125 || t.run.loop_multiplications != 4)
        throw std::runtime_error("exact dyadic witness");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| update | N before | D before | factor | N after | D after |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 0.375 | 0.5 | 1.5 | 0.5625 | 0.75 |
| 2 | 0.5625 | 0.75 | 1.25 | 0.703125 | 0.9375 |
\image(figures/goldschmidt_flow.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The first actual update fans one rounded factor into two products. Both table rows are exact binary-fraction calculations.)
\alttext(One actual correction factor feeds separate numerator and denominator multiplication boxes with the captured numerical values.)

The diagram labels come from the first captured native update. The two-update
estimate is 0.703125, below the exact quotient 0.75; the explicit budget stops
before convergence.

> Derive the exact-arithmetic model

Write d=s·m·2ᵉ, with s∈{−1,+1} and 1/2≤m<1. The core computes D₀=m and
N₀=ldexp(n,−e), then repeats

$$f_t=2-D_t,\qquad N_{t+1}=N_t f_t,\qquad D_{t+1}=D_t f_t.$$
\alttext(The factor is two minus the denominator; both next states multiply their previous state by this same factor.)

In exact arithmetic the ratio Nₜ/Dₜ remains n/|d|. With δₜ=1−Dₜ,

$$\delta_{t+1}=1-D_t(2-D_t)=(1-D_t)^2=\delta_t^2,\qquad
\delta_t=\delta_0^{2^t}.$$
\alttext(The denominator gap squares at every exact-arithmetic update, giving the initial gap raised to two to the update number.)

Thus the signed numerator estimate obeys q̂ₜ=q(1−δₜ), where q=n/d. For q≠0 its
exact relative error is δₜ. Since 0<δ₀≤1/2, the ideal gap shrinks at least as
fast as 2^(−2ᵗ). These identities assume exact scaling and products; the
floating-point implementation does not promise them.

The ordinary API makes at most 60 updates and tests |D−1|<10⁻¹⁶ after each
update. Each completed update counts two multiplications. That selected count
excludes normalization, factor subtraction, tests, the final sign and all
machine/compiler costs. It supports no ranking against hardware division or
Newton iteration.

> Retain the original four divisions

The following program calls the shared native loop. Its hardware quotient is an
independent floating-point comparison, not a replacement for the reported
result. Absolute differences are meaningful for these four ordinary finite
cases. The figure follows the actual 22/7 records; a zero gap is printed in the
table and omitted from the logarithmic native curve, with no artificial floor.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/goldschmidt_gap.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/goldschmidt_division.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::print("| n | d | native quotient | hardware n/d | absolute difference | updates | loop products | " "stop |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (const auto[n, d] : std::vector<std::pair<double, double>>{{1, 3},
                                                                   {22, 7},
                                                                   {-5, 1024},
                                                                   {2, 1e-6}}) {
        const auto r = dm::algorithms::goldschmidt_run(n, d);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {:.17g} | {:.17g} | {:.6g} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
                   n, d, r.quotient, n / d, std::abs(r.quotient - n / d),
                   r.updates, r.loop_multiplications,
                   dm::algorithms::goldschmidt_stop_name(r.stop));
    }
    const auto t = dm::algorithms::goldschmidt_trace(22, 7);
    std::vector<double> x{0}, gap{std::log10(1 - t.run.initial.denominator)}, ix{0}, ideal{gap[0]};
    std::print("\n| update | actual N | actual D | actual gap |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 0 | " "{:.17g} | {:.17g} | {:.17g} |\n",
               t.run.initial.numerator, t.run.initial.denominator,
               1 - t.run.initial.denominator);
    for (const auto &q : t.steps) {
        const double g = 1 - q.after.denominator;
        std::print("| {} | {:.17g} | {:.17g} | {:.17g} |\n", q.update,
                   q.after.numerator, q.after.denominator, g);
        if (g > 0) {
            x.push_back(q.update);
            gap.push_back(std::log10(g));
        }
        ix.push_back(q.update);
        ideal.push_back(std::ldexp(ideal.front(), q.update));
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(ix, ideal, "exact model", {5, 150, 105}, 2).points(
        x, gap, "positive native gap", {37, 99, 235}, 5).title(
        "22 / 7: zero native gap at update 5 is omitted").x_label(
        "completed updates").y_label("log10(1 - D)").save_svg(
        "build/goldschmidt_gap.svg");
    if (t.run.updates != 5 || t.run.final_state.denominator != 1)
        throw std::runtime_error("native stop");
}
```
