\image(figures/goldschmidt_gap.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Native positive gaps are plotted without a floor; the exact-arithmetic curve continues to update five, where the native gap has rounded to zero.)
\alttext(Green exact-model log gap descends quadratically. Blue positive native gaps end at update four because update five has a zero gap.)

For 22/7, D₀=7/8 and the ideal gaps are (1/8)^(2ᵗ). The actual denominator
rounds to one at update five, so execution stops there; no sixth update is
replayed. Agreement with ordinary division on a few examples establishes neither
correct rounding nor accuracy for all representable magnitudes.

> Test where the exact model stops explaining the implementation

For d=nextafter(1,0), normalization leaves D just below one. The correction 2−D
rounds to exactly one in round-to-nearest arithmetic. Every update leaves both
states unchanged. The positive gap 2⁻⁵³ exceeds 10⁻¹⁶, so the loop exhausts all
60 updates.

A separate failure occurs before the loop: dividing the smallest positive
subnormal by one scales its numerator by 1/2, rounding it to zero. The true
hardware quotient remains representable. The final denominator reaches one, but
the native quotient is zero. Finite inputs can also overflow during scaling,
producing infinity. These controls describe the preserved algorithm rather than
silently substituting a more accurate division.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/goldschmidt_limits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/goldschmidt_division.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const double tiny = std::numeric_limits<double>::denorm_min();
    const std::vector<std::pair<double, double>> cases{{22, 7},
                                                       {1,
                                                        std::nextafter(1., 0.)},
                                                       {tiny, 1},
                                                       {std::numeric_limits<
                                                        double>::max(), tiny}};
    std::vector<double> index, work;
    std::print("| case | initial N | final D | native | hardware | updates | stop |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | " "---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    const std::vector<std::string> names{"ordinary", "stalled",
                                         "subnormal loss", "overflow"};
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < cases.size(); ++i) {
        const auto[n, d] = cases[i];
        const auto r = dm::algorithms::goldschmidt_run(n, d);
        index.push_back(i + 1);
        work.push_back(r.loop_multiplications);
        std::print("| {}: {} | {:.17g} | {:.17g} | {:.17g} | {:.17g} | {} | {} |\n",
                   i + 1, names[i], r.initial.numerator,
                   r.final_state.denominator, r.quotient, n / d, r.updates,
                   dm::algorithms::goldschmidt_stop_name(r.stop));
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.bars(index, work, "loop products", {37, 99, 235}).title(
        "A stalled denominator spends the full update budget").x_label(
        "case: 1 ordinary; 2 stall; 3 subnormal; 4 overflow").y_label(
        "selected multiplications (2 per update)").y_limits(0, 155).save_svg(
        "build/goldschmidt_limits.svg");
    const auto stall =
        dm::algorithms::goldschmidt_trace(1, std::nextafter(1., 0.));
    if (stall.run.updates != 60 || stall.steps.back().factor != 1 ||
        dm::algorithms::goldschmidt_division(tiny, 1) != 0)
        throw std::runtime_error("rounding controls");
    bool quota = false, domain = false;
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::goldschmidt_trace(22, 7, 60, 4);
    } catch (const std::length_error &) {
        quota = true;
    }
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::goldschmidt_division(1, -0.);
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument &) {
        domain = true;
    }
    const auto empty = dm::algorithms::goldschmidt_trace(3, 4, 0, 1);
    const auto unlimited = dm::algorithms::goldschmidt_trace(22, 7, 60, 0);
    auto copy = unlimited;
    copy.steps[0].factor = 0;
    std::print("\nQuota rejects fifth record: {}; signed-zero divisor rejected: {}; zero-budget history: " "{}; disabled quota records: {}; copied history independent: {}.\n",
               quota, domain, empty.steps.size(), unlimited.steps.size(),
               unlimited.steps[0].factor != copy.steps[0].factor);
    if (!quota || !domain || !empty.steps.empty() || unlimited.steps.size() != 5)
        throw std::runtime_error("boundary controls");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| case | initial N | final D | native | hardware | updates | stop |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
| 1: ordinary | 2.75 | 1 | 3.1428571428571428 | 3.1428571428571428 | 5 | tolerance |
| 2: stalled | 1 | 0.99999999999999989 | 1 | 1.0000000000000002 | 60 | iteration_limit |
| 3: subnormal loss | 0 | 1 | 0 | 4.9406564584124654e-324 | 6 | tolerance |
| 4: overflow | inf | 1 | inf | inf | 6 | tolerance |

Quota rejects fifth record: true; signed-zero divisor rejected: true; zero-budget history: 0; disabled quota records: 5; copied history independent: true.
\image(figures/goldschmidt_limits.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Case numbers match the table. Only update multiplications are counted; the full 60-step stall costs 120 of them.)
\alttext(Four bars compare actual selected product counts: ordinary ten, stalled one hundred twenty, subnormal twelve and overflow twelve.)

The trace owns one complete record per actual update, with initial normalization
stored separately. The default record quota is one million; zero disables that
policy cap. A next record is admitted before its arithmetic, and exceeding the
cap throws rather than returning a truncated history. Copies own independent
storage. Scalar and work-only calls allocate no history.

Both signed-zero divisors and nonfinite operands are rejected before
normalization. A negative update budget is invalid; an explicit zero budget
normalizes and returns the signed initial numerator without updates. Signed-zero
numerators are accepted. Ordinary floating-point flags and errno are not rolled
back on failure. These examples assume the normal round-to-nearest environment;
the implementation uses the caller's floating-point environment.

> What the evidence supports

The exact recurrence explains rapid denominator convergence while arithmetic
remains faithful to its model. Actual records expose the point where rounding
dominates, and selected operation counts expose wasted updates when the
correction stalls. Neither the denominator stopping test nor the historical
motivation is a quotient-accuracy or speed guarantee. The small constructed
controls are more informative here than an unrelated dataset: each isolates a
specific arithmetic mechanism.

> Source and verification

Robert E. Goldschmidt's 1964 MIT M.S. thesis, [Applications of division by
convergence](https://hdl.handle.net/1721.1/11113), is the historical primary
source. The recurrence above is derived explicitly for this library's
normalization, update order, stopping test and finite-precision behavior. No
claim is made that the thesis specifies this exact software implementation.

The replacement uses the same native kernel for scalar output, work metadata and
capture. A frozen pre-conversion implementation is compared bit for bit on
seeded finite binary64 inputs; exact dyadic controls, record reconstruction,
ownership, quota and invalid-input tests accompany sanitizer checks. The report
programs and figures are executed and inspected before the legacy source is
retired.
